<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>NEW READERS / REVIEWERS PLEASE READ THIS SECTION!</strong></span></p>
<p>Hello again! Yeah I'll definitely explain more of the narrative here since there isn't enough space to explain it all.</p>
<p>Sorry it's so much to go through, I just want to give you an exact idea in my mind of the narrative, the ideas I'm going for, etc.</p>
<p>I know it's a lot, but I think it's worth the read to get a full idea of what I'm thinking.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>ANSWERING YOUR QUESTIONS</strong></span></p>
<p>1. The perspective of the article (such as footnotes, interviews, and experiments) will be performed by the use of two specific doctors (we'll call them Dr. A and Dr. B for now) of the facility. As for the article overall, its from the perspective of the Foundation and its process it goes through to contain the SCP discussed. Dr. A and Dr. B will be the ones doing the main research, expressing their thoughts, their feelings, their discoveries of the SCP, the documentation of the SCP, etc.</p>
<p>2. The readers can relate to multiple people within different parts of the article expressed in interviews, footnotes, memos, and logs. You can relate to the pain the D-Class member faces when he is tested to be cut by the SCP, you can relate to the two doctors conducting the research by their conversations when it comes to interviews and audio logs, and you can relate to guards' horrified reaction when the discovery of the SCP was made in the audio log. All of it is done very subtly, but most of the relatability within characters will be from those of the two main researchers, Dr. A and Dr. B.</p>
<p>3. So there is quite a bit to the entire story around the SCP from the time it was discovered to the final documentation that the viewer reads from the article. It goes in the order of:</p>
<p>Discovery of the SCP by guards, MTF, and Dr. K,<br />
the investigation and studying of the SCP's goop by The Foundation &amp; Dr. A,<br />
the experiment of D-Class by Dr. B,<br />
the initial and additional testing results of the SCP by Dr. A,<br />
the sudden disappearance of Dr. A mid-way through testing,<br />
then the rest of the documentation and testing being performed by Dr. B.</p>
<p>More in-depth explanation is in the bottom portion of this response.</p>
<hr />
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>BETTER EXPLANATION OF THE ANOMALY DUE TO LACK OF TEXT SPACE IN THE POST</strong></span></p>
<p>So imagine a little sponge in the shape of a cute squid. It looks almost like a child's toy but feels and looks like a sponge due to the holes/texture it has.</p>
<p>It has a dark yellow hole at the bottom center. This hole is just that, a textured hole with nothing sharp or anything inside it. This dark yellow hole is where the SCP cuts its victims.</p>
<p>An example of what the SCP looks like (without the spongy texture and look) are here:<br />
<a href="https://www.etsy.com/listing/762422149/cute-small-aquatic-squid-plush-toy?gpla=1&amp;gao=1&amp;">https://www.etsy.com/listing/762422149/cute-small-aquatic-squid-plush-toy?gpla=1&amp;gao=1&amp;</a><br />
and<br />
<a href="https://www.etsy.com/listing/400028871/little-squid-baby-baby-squid-blushing">https://www.etsy.com/listing/400028871/little-squid-baby-baby-squid-blushing</a></p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">There are four anomalous factors of the SCP</span></p>
<p><strong>1. It manages to suddenly cut (multiple times) any organism that enters its hole.</strong> Although <em>the hole itself has no sharp objects to speak of</em>, it manages to cut any individual that enters/puts its body part in the hole.</p>
<p>The cuts from the SCP's hole varies at about 4-20 cuts, with depths of the cuts as entire slices of the thing that entered the SCP's hole.</p>
<p>Here's an example: If a person put their finger in the hole of the SCP, then that person's would be cut multiple times. The cuts would be through the entire finger from top to bottom.</p>
<p>An example image of the hole that the SCP has (obviously not to scale as the SCP is quite small) can be found here <a href="https://reductress.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/01/hole.jpg">https://reductress.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/01/hole.jpg</a>.</p>
<p><strong>2. The SCP has a restriction, in that it can only cut an organism (or an organism's body part) within its hole if it is alive (or part of an alive organism).</strong></p>
<p>For instance, a finger of a <em>dead</em> rotting corpse <em>would not be cut</em> inside the hole of this SCP because a rotting corpse is well&#8230; dead: it has no living qualities.</p>
<p>On the other hand, a finger of a regular <em>alive</em> person inside the hole of the SCP <em>would get cut</em> because the finger is part of an alive human being.</p>
<p>This doesn't just retain to body parts of organisms however. If a small (<em>alive</em>) entity, such as a lizard, entered the SCP's hole, then it <em>would be cut</em> as well.</p>
<p><strong>3. If an organism or an organism's body part has a nail (the anatomy definition of nail) and enters the SCP's hole, the organism will have all of its nails fall/cut off.</strong></p>
<p>For example, lets go back to the use of an alive human's finger. Since fingers actually have a nail at its tip, if it were to enter the hole of the SCP, then all of that human's nails would fall off.</p>
<p><strong>4. The SCP leaks a dangerous goo after cutting an entity</strong></p>
<p>Within the span of 1 - 5 minutes after cutting, the SCP will omit a strange goo from its body.<br />
Similar to how a soapy sponge squeezes out soap and water, the SCP shall leak out a goo from its body in a high-rate.</p>
<p>This goo is dark orange and when any organic being touches it, it slowly designates to dust, starting from the source it was touched.</p>
<p>The goo does NOT affect any form of separate material (I.E it is not connected to a being), so the use of mittens, gloves, and protection gear is advised when this is realized.</p>
<p>Ex 1: A person's finger touching the goo would slowly disintegrate them, starting from their finger.</p>
<p>Ex 2: A person's finger who's entire hand is inside an rubber-glove when touching the goo does not disintegrate, since it is protected from the fact that the rubber glove is not alive or biologically connected to something alive.</p>
<hr />
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>CENTRAL NARRATIVE EXPLANATION (THE ARC) FROM ADDENDUMS AND LOGS</strong></span></p>
<p><em>NOTE: Dr. A and Dr. B were made to help better express that the foundation doesn't simply have one person studying SCPs. It also gives a good way into giving more mystery found in the SCP. The list here is to get a better in-depth look of each part of the narrative.</em></p>
<p><strong>a. <em>Discovery</em></strong> - Discovery of the SCP is made so readers can better understand how it is found (it is already explained above what happens and where). The discovery ends at the point of where MTF manage to take in the SCP for studying. It is later discovered from investigation and studying that Dr. K had not disappeared but he actually disintegrated due to the leaking goo from the SCP.</p>
<p><strong><em>Audio Log</em></strong> - Here it gives another perspective, in this case from the use of audio, to the discovery of the SCP by Dr. K. You hear (or in this case from the document, read) all sounds from this perspective. You can hear (basically read in the audio-log) the actions Dr. K makes, the pain Dr. K has when he is hurt by the SCP, the slushing of liquids from the SCP in the bathroom, the screaming from the guards when they find the pile of blood in the bathroom, the screaming/commands of the MTF during the capture of the SCP, and the general fear of what goes on from the SCP's discovery.</p>
<p><strong>b. <em>Investigation &amp; Studying Of Discovery</em></strong> - Here you read about the investigation of the goop that was found where the SCP was discovered (this case being the bathroom and sink-pipes). It is here that the Foundation goes into their process of removing pipes and their process they performed to force staff to use different materials from other site facilities.</p>
<p>The foundation believes that the goop came from the SCP, and the foundation now requires routine checks of the bathrooms by MTF and an &quot;object list&quot; for staff to identify objects that aren't of the usual. It is here that things such as the routine check and &quot;allowed objects list&quot; come from.</p>
<p>It is also the beginning of the research from Dr. A.<br />
Dr. A has been told to discover what properties the goop has, and so he does testing of the goo with alive-beings (in this case, lizards).</p>
<p>Dr. A explains that the SCP's goop has a few identifiable qualities:<br />
- It smells like pee<br />
- It is extremely sticky<br />
- It has a dark color orange color<br />
- Any alive-being that touches it manages to fade to dust</p>
<p><strong>c. <em>Experimentation / Studies</em></strong> - Here is where we get more insight into researcher Dr. B. He performs an experiment of the SCP with the use of D-Class. The D-Class is told to hold the SCP, hold it at specific angles, shake the SCP, and put his finger in the SCP.</p>
<p>The D-Class is cut, and the results are documented by Dr. B. It is here that it is identified what some of the SCP's anomolous properties are to humans.</p>
<p><strong>d. <em>Testing Chart</em></strong> - After experimentation, more tests are performed by Dr. A to see what exactly the SCP would cut from its hole. The results are put into a table chart and are put onto the SCP article. It is documented that the SCP seems to only cut beings that are alive or connected parts of an alive organism's body.</p>
<p>All testing of the SCP occurred through-out the span of multiple days according to the chart. The chart explains what item was being tested, the day an item was tested, and if cutting occurred from the SCP.</p>
<p><strong>f. <em>Footnotes</em></strong> - Here it is explained that Dr. A had actually disappeared from touching the goo, which can be assumed occured for Dr. K as well, during the testing of the SCP.</p>
<p>These bits of the footnotes just adds more explanation to why certain members go missing through-out this story.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p><em>Enjoy!</em></p>

