			<div class="content" id="post-content-4818964">
									

<p>Hmmm, I can definitely see where you're seeing from.</p>
<p>So if I give a reasoning or finding to their disappearance, would the reader feel less cheated?<br />
Cause one of my ideas is to add ambiguity to leave horror to the viewer's imagination.</p>
<p>My ideas (1-4):</p>
<p>1. If we're giving reasoning to their disappearance, maybe we could do it by having both Dr. K's and Dr. A's bodies be found later on in a sink/bathroom/drain-pipe? Therefore it would have an explanation as to where they went (it can be assumed they died somehow from the SCP), but the reader can still use their imagination because of how vague it is. I could also add in that the Foundation is looking into further research as to whether their deaths were linked to the SCP, and that they're going to do further testing in the future.</p>
<p>2. Rather than the idea above, I could also go ahead and say that as of a specific date, the Foundation (in this case, Dr. B) had realized that the SCP has its users die days later as one of the anomalous affects of being cut.</p>
<p>3. Or instead of any of these ideas above, we could just have the &quot;goo&quot; (the slush found with the SCP) be a leaking substance from the SCP, in which anyone who comes in contact with this goo will eventually turn into that same goop. This would explain their &quot;disappearance&quot; and so the reader wouldn't feel cheated.</p>
<p>4. We cut the ambiguity entirely and change the narrative to where the bodies don't go missing. I know it'll remove the ambiguity, but maybe there might not be a way to incorporate these parts of &quot;disappearing bodies&quot; without it being out-of-place.</p>
<p>So what would you recommend? I would love to hear how you could fix this issue, and if you could somehow find a better way to incorporate ambiguity into your explanation.</p>

							</div>

								
